{"body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text' and 'an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own.' The two replies are, first, '[{\"a\": 1}, {\"b\": 2}]' and, second, '{\"b\": 2}'. For the first reply, there is no code fence and no top-level object: the only objects are elements of an array, which are not top-level, so the rule requires recover_json_object to raise RecordError with 'no JSON object could be recovered from the response'. For the second reply, the last top-level object is '{\"b\": 2}', so the rule requires it to return ({\"b\": 2}, ()). The check gives the same answer for both: it returns ({\"b\": 2}, ()) for the first reply because its top-level scanner calls raw_decode at each '{' without excluding objects inside arrays, so it treats '{\"b\": 2}' as a top-level candidate. I would be wrong if recover_json_object on '[{\"a\": 1}, {\"b\": 2}]' raises RecordError instead of returning '{\"b\": 2}', or if it returns any answer other than ({\"b\": 2}, ()) for that text."}